---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SurveyDesignPrinciples from "../components/SurveyDesignPrinciples.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Survey"
  description="The MISTY Survey: a resolved view of the baryon cycle across the low-metallicity regime."
>
  <article class="survey-page">
    <div class="survey-page__intro">
      <div class="survey-page__prose">
        <p class="survey-page__lead">
          MISTY is designed to establish a resolved view of the baryon cycle
          across the low-metallicity regime. Low-mass galaxies dominate the
          galaxy population by number, and the physical conditions that govern
          their formation and evolution differ fundamentally from those in massive,
          near-solar-metallicity systems. Metal-poor massive stars emit harder
          ionizing radiation; reduced dust and metal content alter shielding,
          cooling, and chemistry; and shallow gravitational potentials allow stellar
          feedback to redistribute or expel gas more efficiently.
        </p>
        <p>
          Over the past decade, resolved multi-wavelength surveys have
          transformed our understanding of the baryon cycle in nearby massive
          galaxies. By mapping molecular clouds, H&nbsp;II regions, and young
          star clusters on 10–100&nbsp;pc scales across entire galaxies, these
          programs have constrained cloud lifetimes, gas–star overlap,
          pre-supernova cloud dispersal, and variations in star-formation
          efficiency. MISTY aims to provide the comprehensive counterpart for 
          the low-mass, low-metallicity regime, where these processes remain far
          less well constrained.
        </p>
        <p>
          MISTY is designed around two defining features of the baryon cycle: it operates
          across a wide range of spatial scales and links stars, multiphase gas, and dust.
          Understanding it therefore demands a multi-scale, multi-wavelength approach.
          The formation and evolution of young star clusters, H&nbsp;II-region structure,
          cloud dispersal, metal enrichment, and turbulence injection occur on scales of
          approximately 10–100&nbsp;pc, while their cumulative effects on gas flows,
          ionizing-radiation escape, galaxy-wide star formation, and chemical evolution
          must be measured across entire galaxies. Characterizing stars, multiphase gas, 
          and dust further requires observations across the electromagnetic spectrum. 
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="survey-page__principles">
      <SurveyDesignPrinciples />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .survey-page {
    max-width: var(--reading-max);
    /* Combined with .site-main padding-top (~1.5rem): ~44–48px total */
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .survey-page__prose {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.68;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .survey-page__lead {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .survey-page__prose p {
    margin-bottom: 1.15em;
  }

  .survey-page__prose p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .survey-page__principles {
    margin-top: 3rem; /* ~48px mobile separation */
    padding-top: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .survey-page {
      /* Combined with .site-main padding-top (~1.85rem): ~56px total */
      padding-top: 1.65rem;
    }

    .survey-page__prose {
      font-size: 1.125rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
    }

    .survey-page__lead {
      font-size: 1.1875rem;
      line-height: 1.72;
    }

    .survey-page__principles {
      margin-top: 4.5rem; /* ~72px desktop separation */
    }
  }
</style>
